[OTHERS] Okay, anytime. [INTERVIEWEE] Do you can they play it back for you, that little piece? [OTHERS] No, we haven't switch. No, no, no. No. [INTERVIEWER] You mentioned that on the night of November 10th, they entered your town. And that there were people visiting at your house at the time. Would you go on from there? [INTERVIEWEE] It was November 9th, the evening of my father's birthday, and some friends had come in. And uh, the next morning, uh, the uh, the SS came to take him away. and they referred to the murder that the shooting that had occurred in Paris when Herschel Grynszpan shot the German embassy official because he was so upset about the uh letter he received from his parents who had also been sent back to the eastern borders and had no place to go. [INTERVIEWER] Now, what happened to the men at this time? [INTERVIEWEE] Uh, they were taken away. As I said, my father was stayed in jail. Um, the day progressed and of course, people were phoning each other who was taken and who wasn't. There were a few men who didn't happen to be home or perhaps they had heard rumors and disappeared, but for the most part, they were all taken, I think down to age 14, uh, young, young boys up to that age. Uh, that night, my mother yanked me out of bed in the middle of the night and I, I heard this terrible banging against our door, and she pulled me out of bed and we went upstairs. We had a large house and we had tenants on the third floor, non-Jewish tenants, and um, we went to their apartment and they hid us. And we also had a young Jewish girl who was our housekeeper, uh, she was living in the fourth floor and she came down and we all hid in our tenant's apartment. And all I can remember there was this terrible crashing and and and breaking noises. They were breaking through the windows and trying to hack down the doors and and breaking glass, and they were just vandalizing. And uh our tenant went downstairs and said, look, I live on the upper floor, so just curtail your activities to the first floor. And they took his word for it. Um, and they just rampaged through through the rooms, they destroyed pictures and furniture and pulled the book built-in bookcases out of the walls and tore the books. I mean, it was just unabashed vandalism. Uh, they stayed and rampaged around, they threw eggs and and other foods in between all the furnishings and what not. And in the mornings, they they stood guard yet in front of the house until daybreak to make sure we wouldn't leave. They pulled the gas range out of the wall and the gas was escaping throughout the house, but the windows were broken and luckily nobody lit a cigarette. Um, that's what occurred and and I just, my mother tells me that at one point she had a hard time holding me back because I wanted to run down there and beg them to stop and they would have probably killed me if I had done that. I don't remember that, but she tells me I did that. [INTERVIEWER] You made mention that your father was taken to a local prison and many of the men were sent to Buchenwald and to Dachau. [INTERVIEWEE] Yes. [INTERVIEWER] And I asked you, had you heard of either of these camps prior to the men being sent at this time? [INTERVIEWEE] I can't say that I did. But afterwards, the names became very familiar. Sachsenhausen was another one that I remember, uh, have hearing hearing the name of. Those three were the ones that I heard the names of after Crystal night. Also, uh, when all this breakage was going, they were the sirens were heard because they had set the synagogue on fire and our school was on the same premise as the synagogue. That too was vandalized. The synagogue was just a shell after the fire was extinguished. [INTERVIEWER] Now, what after Crystal night did you hear about these camps? Do you remember? [INTERVIEWEE] No, they were terrible, but the men weren't allowed to talk about it. They had to sign that they wouldn't speak about the camps and what happened there on punishment of death and that they would, they were required when they were released to sign that they would leave within a short period of time and never return to Germany on on punishment of death and that's why, uh, what happened later became so critical because of that. [INTERVIEWER] Now, your father is in the local prison. How long do they keep him in prison? [INTERVIEWEE] I think they kept him six weeks, my mother said. A policeman got in touch with my mother one night. and she tells me this. and told her to meet him at night in the in a quiet street. most of the streets were quiet in that town at night anyway in those days. And he told her that he had access to my father and that he was well and that he needed some fresh underwear and my father was on certain diet that he needed some of his medication and things like that and he was willing to bring it to him. And my mother and he kept in constant touch between my mother and my father. He also called her up to tell her when he was coming out and she was able to convey to my father where she was since we weren't living in the house anymore. Uh after Crystal night, my mother took me away from the town to Bonn, where we had Dutch nationals, friends who were Dutch nationals, and she felt I would be safe in their house because they weren't German Jews, they were Dutch. And she went back to try and get my father out of jail. She lived with friends, and she told the policeman to tell him where to come, not to go to the house. She didn't want him to see it. It was boarded up. But she tells me he went there first anyway. [INTERVIEWER] Now what was your life like? [INTERVIEWEE] And when he came back, he just broke down when he saw the house. and the house looked that morning like a bomb pitted. I've seen houses where bomb hits. It looked like a bomb pitted. [INTERVIEWER] During the time that your father was in jail and you were living with these friends in Bonn, what was your life like at that time? and what was life like at Bonn at that time? [INTERVIEWEE] Um, I lived with wonderful people. There were quite a few people living in this house, um, but no children. Uh, however, the Jewish school in Bonn had not been destroyed because it didn't look like a school. See, many of the vandals who did the uh carnage on November 9th and 10th came from different towns. That was purposefully done so that nobody could point a finger at their Gentile neighbors say, you did it, you were the ones. And they didn't know that this building was a school. It looked more like a storefront from the outside. You couldn't see the schoolyard from the street. And so the school was still in session and I went to school. Uh, and the Canter's daughter there befriended me, and he was such a wonderful, sensitive man and realized that as an only child, I was quite bewildered having been torn away from my parents and I spent a lot more time with his daughter at his house than I did at the people's house where I lived because, you know, they made me feel comfortable. They were like my, my sub, uh, my substitute parents during that time. [INTERVIEWER] And what was the family name? The Canter's name? [INTERVIEWEE] Winterberg. The name was Winterberg. Um, this girl was also an only child. Eventually, she went to she and her parents went to Theresienstadt. from there to Auschwitz. She is the only survivor and today she lives 10 minutes away from my house. [INTERVIEWER] How wonderful. [INTERVIEWEE] Yeah, I think it is wonderful. [INTERVIEWER] Now, when your father finally was released from prison, what happened to your family then? [INTERVIEWEE] Well, first of all, I think they tried to arrange for the repair of the house and to to they had to sell it. Um, my parents were friendly with Cuban people who had uh connections with the Cuban consulate. And one of these people had gone home to Cuba and came back with these landing permits that was supposed to be immigration certificates. Uh they had bought them for us figuring that we would be anxious to have them. And so that is uh and and also the news of the St. Louis having been scheduled for this trip seem to come simultaneously to this event. And that that way my parents were getting ready then to leave Germany. [INTERVIEWER] Now, you were all in Bonn at the time? [INTERVIEWEE] No, I was in Bonn, my parents were in Rheydt and I returned to Rheydt only for a few days to say goodbye to my friends who were still there. And I have to tell you a small incident, I went to say goodbye to uh, the teacher and his children. and I spent an afternoon with them. They were a little older than myself, but that didn't matter at that time. We were playing cards and um, the boy taught me how to shuffle like the the uh gamblers do. And while he was doing that, he said, you know Hitler says that by 1945, there will be no Jews left alive in Europe. And every time I play cards and shuffle the cards, I remember that. This family didn't make it. My teacher and his family were wiped out. It's just an incident that stays with me. [INTERVIEWER] When did you first hear about the voyage that was going to take place on the St. Louis? [INTERVIEWEE] Sometime during my stay in Bonn, my parents came to visit me, um, frequently. [INTERVIEWER] When did they first, do you know anything about how they first heard about it? [INTERVIEWEE] I don't know exactly when the period of time between Crystal night and May 13th is approximately half a year, somewhere in that period of time these events took place, but I can't pinpoint it. [INTERVIEWER] From the time you found out about it, how did the events occur? [INTERVIEWEE] Well, from the time I found out about it, my mother said on such and such a day, I'll come and get you, you'll come back to Rheydt, we'll get ready and we'll go to Berlin. My my mother's one sister had emigrated to Holland, the other sister lived in Berlin. She was a doctor with her family, she had one son and her husband was a uh pianist and um they were the ones who had that 