's a folk song, which means must must I leave this little place? And again, when I hear it, it brings back the memory. [INTERVIEWER] The captain and the crew were from what country? [INTERVIEWEE] Oh, they were German. They were German. The captain, I think lived in Hamburg. [INTERVIEWER] What was the captain's name on the boat? [INTERVIEWEE] Uh Joseph Schroeder was his name. Uh the captain, the um I remember also my mother's sister had come with us, you see, and she was standing on the docks and we were up on deck, and I was only, I wasn't quite 11, but I knew fairly certain I would never see her again. And I'm certain that my parents knew that too. [INTERVIEWER] I'm sure that was true of a lot of people that were on the docks at the time. [INTERVIEWEE] Yes. Yes. [INTERVIEWER] As you entered the boat, you said that there was a scene of a very festive scene in terms of being a cruise liner and the captain wanting to maintain this. What events occurred after you got on board the boat? [INTERVIEWEE] Uh well, we went, were shown to our cabin. Our cabin was across from the purser's office. and he came and introduced himself and said that if there anything that we wanted, we should let him know. and um I, being a a normal kid of that age, I explored to see what this ship was like. I'd never been on such a large ship. And uh I think in general, the mood was high, it was very positive. Uh aside from the fact that we had to leave so many loved people uh behind, uh the mood in general was optimistic. Uh as a matter of fact, we were expecting to uh we did stop in Holland to pick up some passengers and my father's sister was supposed to come on board with her two sons. The boys had been sent to Holland in previous years. And uh my father had bought passage for his sister and her family as well. She was a widow and they didn't come. and we were quite worried about them and she wrote that she hadn't sold her house yet. She'd come on the next boat, but I'm afraid that decision cost her her life and the life of one of her sons. [OTHERS] Okay, right there we're going to change tapes. [INTERVIEWER] So after you [OTHERS] are we ready? Uh, just a few more moments. [INTERVIEWER] Okay. [OTHERS] Okay. Anytime. [INTERVIEWER] After you finished exploring the ship and people settled down to the cruise, what generally was the talk that was amongst the adults on the ship? [INTERVIEWEE] I would say probably uh conjecture of what was ahead for them, how long or how short of a time they expected to spend in Cuba, who was meeting them. Lots of the people who were on board were half of a family where for instance, mostly the father had gone ahead to New York or to Havana and was going to wait for the family and pick them up or other relatives whom they expected to meet, what they might do in the interim of time. They offered Spanish lessons on board. Um, and mostly I wanted to be with kids, you know, and didn't hang around the adults too much. But uh in general, the people were just enjoying the relaxation of the cruise. They they were getting food that we hadn't seen in Germany for a long time. In Germany, you got a Zatz coffee and the the white bread was gray. you know, things like that had no longer quality. The Germans were apparently already on a uh kind of a restrictive program as far as that was concerned and uh here there was a much freer atmosphere, you know, the meals were good, you could play uh the deck sports or go to a gym or have a swim in the pool once we were in warmer climbs or and there were children's activities because of so many children, they had a program for the kids all the time. [INTERVIEWER] Now, how many tea dances? [INTERVIEWEE] People were being frivolous because they had been under such terrible pressures and especially where the men had been incarcerated. I think it was really the first breath of fresh air any of them had taken in years, I would say, in years. [INTERVIEWER] What was the total number of people on the boat when 