ery quickly, and most of the gentlemen were attorneys, there were two doctors and uh two non-professionals in the group. [INTERVIEWER] Now, what did this group, what was this this group for? [INTERVIEWEE] Well, they first, they first met with the captain and I think he discussed in general that he had heard these rumors that in case there would actually be a problem, he would need people, first of all, to assure the passengers to keep calm because uh very quickly a terrible uh unrest or even panic could take place on a board a ship you're very limited. Uh you don't know where it's going to lead and I think he was just a very wise leader this captain in thinking of any eventuality that might come to pass. Uh, also he felt that there would be negotiations that would have to take place and he expressed to these men right initially, right from the start, that it was his job and his duty to deliver his guests to their destination. [INTERVIEWER] Now how many days were you from Cuba when he called this committee together? [INTERVIEWEE] I couldn't tell you for sure. Uh, perhaps five days. Perhaps five days. [INTERVIEWER] Was the rest of the voyage after this committee was formed a good voyage without incident or any moves? [INTERVIEWEE] It was, yes, but but you see, I heard my father talk to my mother and they had meetings off and on, you know, and there was a a a an aura of um a mild anxiety, shall I say, uh on from what I could hear. From what I could hear. But generally speaking, the passengers were not aware of that. [INTERVIEWER] Now you're finally approaching Cuba. Yes. What with what was the feeling on board boat when you Cuba was sighted? [INTERVIEWEE] Oh, I think it was grand anticipation. I remember it was early in the morning when we uh when we approached Havana and um the uh the uh Harbor patrol came on board and the medical officers and people were told to to get their their steamer, you know, people had luggage in the hold. Uh they had steamer trunks in the ro