gs happening behind the scenes that we hadn't the foggiest notion about. Uh, the book tells you uh and that was the first that I knew of it when I read the book that this was really an espionage mission and that this particular activity was being accomplished while we were sitting in the harbor. Uh, this morning, the rabbi spoke in the synagogue about the fact that actually, and this we didn't know either that before the ship ever left Hamburg, the Hapag knew that the passengers weren't going to get off. This was a strictly a propaganda coup for the German government. Nobody wants the Jews, not Cuba, not the United States, not anybody else. [INTERVIEWER] Once you realized you were not disembarking, did your father's committee then begin to organize certain things for the passenger? [INTERVIEWEE] Absolutely. [INTERVIEWER] What were the things that they organized and came to them? [INTERVIEWEE] Well, apparently, they got in touch uh with the American Jewish Joint Distribution Committee in New York, in Paris, in Europe, uh with various other Jewish organizations in Havana, in in mainly in New York. And uh there were negotiations going on from New York. A man by the name of Berenson was sent down to Havana to negotiate. And eventually, um he he got to talk to Brú, President Brú made himself unavailable to everyone. He was in a summer house, he wasn't there. Uh people, Cuban friends whom my father had through the Cuban connection in Germany, tried to uh get to him and it was all he was just not available. And this Mr. Berenson apparently did get to see him and uh the Cuban President uh made the offer finally that if he were to get uh $500 per head for the for the uh passengers, which would guarantee uh their sustenance and that they wouldn't be any kind of uh burdens to the government, to the country, he would allow them to get off. And Mr. Berenson bargained with him because it was usual to bargain with that in that part of the world and that was his big mistake. This t